          "text": "So if the direct neurochemical thesis is weak, what's left? This is where a more interesting — and more defensible — thesis emerges, one that inverts the causal arrow entirely. Call it the mucosal-cognitive axis: the idea that phosphatidylcholine's cognitive effects, where real, are not neurochemical at all. They are architectural. PC's actual, well-evidenced job in the body is structural — it is a load-bearing molecule in the intestinal mucus layer, not a neurotransmitter feedstock.",
