        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp6",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 1681,
            "endChar": 2390
          },
          "text": "Unlike the choline-precursor story, the mucus-layer story has real clinical backing — not universal, not proven-in-general-populations, but real. In ulcerative colitis specifically, PC content in the colonic mucus is measurably depleted relative to healthy tissue, and delayed-release PC formulations have shown genuine efficacy in trials as an adjunct treatment, improving remission rates against placebo. The proposed mechanism is elegantly physical rather than chemical: PC increases the hydrophobicity of the mucus layer, which is what allows that layer to function as a barrier at all — keeping luminal bacteria and their inflammatory byproducts from making direct contact with the epithelium underneath.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp6_51587abf",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp6_de77d1a0"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp7",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 2392,
            "endChar": 2724
          },
          "text": "This is the part of the story that is genuinely established. What's not established — what remains thesis rather than finding — is the next link in the chain: that repairing this barrier in a person without diagnosed inflammatory bowel disease produces a downstream cognitive effect large enough to notice subjectively as \"sharper.\"",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp7_ae8e1bbf",
