          "range": {
            "startChar": 2761,
            "endChar": 3429
          },
          "text": "The gut-brain axis itself is not fringe. Systemic low-grade inflammation, much of it originating from gut permeability, is one of the more robust findings connecting peripheral physiology to central cognitive symptoms — the mechanism runs through circulating inflammatory cytokines and their effects on neuroinflammation, mood circuitry, and subjective mental clarity (\"brain fog\" is not just a folk term; it correlates with inflammatory markers in a growing body of research). So each individual link in the chain — PC repairs mucus, mucus barrier reduces inflammatory signaling, reduced inflammatory signaling improves subjective cognition — has independent support.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp9_a5552fa5",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp9_74e1b3e8"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp10",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 3431,
            "endChar": 3869
          },
          "text": "What has not been directly studied is the chain as a single pathway. Nobody has run the trial that takes a cohort of people without diagnosed gut pathology, gives half of them PC, and measures both intestinal permeability markers and cognitive performance simultaneously to confirm the mediation. The thesis is built by welding together two separately-supported segments of track, not by finding a train that's already run the whole line.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp10_cb1c9069",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp10_f4a6d564"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp11",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 3871,
            "endChar": 3895
          },
          "text": "The reframe that matters",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp11_13c98aaf",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp11_9bedf310"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp12",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 3897,
            "endChar": 4514
          },
          "text": "The interesting move here isn't \"does PC help ADHD\" — that's likely a dead end, chasing a mechanism (cholinergic precursor supply) that doesn't seem to be the actual bottleneck for attention circuitry. The interesting move is recognizing that a molecule can have a real and significant biological effect while that effect operates through a completely different system than the one its marketing invokes. PC's true leverage point may not be neurochemical at all — it may be that it is, structurally, a repair material for a barrier, and barriers, when breached, produce inflammatory noise that the brain reads as fog.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp12_19664421",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp12_ca04fda7"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp13",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 4516,
            "endChar": 4913
          },
          "text": "If there's a breakthrough-shaped thread in this, it's this: the felt experience of \"mental sharpness\" from a supplement is not good evidence for the mechanism the supplement is marketed under. A person can correctly perceive a real cognitive change and completely mis-locate its cause, because the actual causal chain runs through an organ system three steps removed from the one being advertised.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp13_ddd72eb7",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp13_8a556989"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp14",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 4913,
            "endChar": 5347
          },
          "text": "This isn't a story specific to phosphatidylcholine — it's a general epistemic trap in how supplement effects get narrated: physiological plausibility gets borrowed from one well-studied use case (UC-specific mucus repair) and silently transplanted onto an entirely different, untested use case (general cognitive enhancement), with the subjective experience of \"feeling sharper\" serving as false confirmation for the wrong mechanism.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp14_b08a9104",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp14_dda6d7b9"
        },
        {
          "spanId": "sp15",
          "range": {
            "startChar": 5349,
            "endChar": 5910
          },
          "text": "The honest version of the thesis, then, isn't \"PC makes you smarter.\" It's: if PC is doing anything for cognition in someone without diagnosed gut pathology, the most defensible current hypothesis is that it's acting as gut-barrier infrastructure repair with cognition as a distant, inflammation-mediated downstream effect — not as a choline-supply intervention on the brain's own neurotransmitter machinery. That's a smaller, weirder, more interesting claim than the supplement-aisle version, and it happens to be the one actually consistent with the evidence.",
          "voAssetId": "a_sp15_8736fcd2",
          "alignmentAssetId": "al_sp15_38781069"
        }
      ]
    },
