          "createdAt": "2026-08-16T02:04:34.252Z",
          "sourceText": "Unlike the choline-precursor story, the mucus-layer story has real clinical backing — not universal, not proven-in-general-populations, but real. In ulcerative colitis specifically, PC content in the colonic mucus is measurably depleted relative to healthy tissue, and delayed-release PC formulations have shown genuine efficacy in trials as an adjunct treatment, improving remission rates against placebo. The proposed mechanism is elegantly physical rather than chemical: PC increases the hydrophobicity of the mucus layer, which is what allows that layer to function as a barrier at all — keeping luminal bacteria and their inflammatory byproducts from making direct contact with the epithelium underneath."
